  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,\u27fa\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
